"Connects threats to a loss-of-control event and onward consequences, separating preventive controls from recovery controls.\n\nUse when: Discuss which barriers prevent or mitigate a specific operational hazard. Review control ownership, weaknesses and escalation factors.\n\nAvoid when: The team needs detailed logical failure combinations or reliable incident probabilities.\n\nVisual grammar: A central top event denotes loss of control over a hazard. Threat paths approach from the left and consequence paths leave right. Barrier blocks sit on the paths they control; escalation factors explain degradation.\n\nBuild: Define the hazard and precise loss-of-control event separately. Identify credible threats and consequences without mixing their roles. Place preventive and recovery barriers and document their assurance and failure conditions.\n\nCheck: Each barrier addresses a specific path and has an actionable function. The top event is distinct from both cause and ultimate harm. Recovery controls remain on the consequence side.\n\nPitfalls: Listing vague intentions such as be careful as effective barriers. Assuming several controls are independent because they have different labels.\n\nExample: A fuel-transfer analysis connects hose failure to a spill top event, with inspection preventing release and containment reducing consequences.\n\nAlternatives: Fault tree, Event tree."
